[issue_comment | you06 | 2021-09-24T16:35:48Z]
@CycleYe thanks for the cases. This makes sense to me. I have some thoughts:

- Update statements lock all matched visible records.
- Update statements write records whether they need change (I think there is no performance issue since we just change a lock operation to a put operation when committing the transaction).
- In-transaction read statements will merge the results of snapshot rows and in-memory changed buffer.

This will fix the issues in update and insert cases. For the delete case, I do not even know what's the expected behavior since mixed usage of snapshot read and current read is too evil.

